{"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{},"source":["# Stochastic reaction simulation with `chisurf.math.reaction._reaction`\n\nThis notebook demonstrates how to use the Numba-based Gillespie SSA backend in\n`chisurf.math.reaction._reaction` to simulate a simple reversible reaction:\n\n\\[ A \\rightleftharpoons B \\]\n\nWe will:\n\n- define the stoichiometry matrix and propensity functions,\n- construct a `Model` instance,\n- run multiple stochastic trajectories, and\n- visualize the mean population dynamics.\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{},"source":["## Imports and setup\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{},"outputs":[],"source":["import numpy as np\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nfrom chisurf.math.reaction import _reaction\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{},"source":["## Define a simple reversible reaction system\n\nWe consider two species, `A` and `B`, and two reactions:\n\n1. A \\u2192 B with rate constant k1\\n2. B \\u2192 A with rate constant k2\n\nThe state vector is `[n_A, n_B]`. The transition matrix columns contain the\nstate change when each reaction fires:\n\n- R1 (A \\u2192 B): [-1, +1]^T\n- R2 (B \\u2192 A): [+1, -1]^T\n\nPropensities are chosen as mass-action kinetics:\n\n- a1 = k1 * n_A\n- a2 = k2 * n_B\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{},"outputs":[],"source":["# Variable names\nvnames = ['A', 'B']\n\n# Initial molecule counts (state vector)\nini = np.array([100, 0], dtype=float)\n\n# Rate constants [k1, k2]\nrates = np.array([0.05, 0.02], dtype=float)\n\n# Transition matrix tm with shape (n_vars, n_reactions)\n# Column 0: A -> B  => [-1, +1]\n# Column 1: B -> A  => [+1, -1]\ntm = np.array([\n    [-1.0, +1.0],  # changes in A\n    [+1.0, -1.0],  # changes in B\n])\n\n# Define propensity functions using the new backend's helpers as a template\n# Here we define them explicitly to reflect A <-> B reactions.\n\ndef prop_forward(r, x):\n    """A -> B: k1 * n_A"""\n    return float(r[0] * x[0])\n\n\ndef prop_backward(r, x):\n    """B -> A: k2 * n_B"""\n    return float(r[1] * x[1])\n\npropensity = [prop_forward, prop_backward]\n\nmodel = _reaction.Model(\n    vnames=vnames,\n    rates=rates,\n    inits=ini,\n    tmat=tm,\n    propensity=propensity,\n)\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{},"source":["## Run Gillespie SSA trajectories\n\nWe now run several SSA trajectories up to a fixed maximum time index `tmax`.\nThe backend uses an internal Numba-accelerated loop when Numba is available,\nand falls back to pure Python otherwise.\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{},"outputs":[],"source":["tmax = 200  # number of time steps stored in the trajectory array\nreps = 200   # number of stochastic repetitions\n\nmodel.run(method='SSA', tmax=tmax, reps=reps)\n\ntime, series, steps = model.getStats()\n\n# series has shape (tmax, n_vars, reps)\nprint('time shape :', time.shape)\nprint('series shape:', series.shape)\nprint('total steps in last run:', steps)\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","metadata":{},"source":["## Plot mean populations over time\n\nWe can compute the mean counts of A and B across all repetitions as a\nfunction of the discrete time index stored by the algorithm.\n"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":null,"metadata":{},"outputs":[],"source":["mean_A = series[:, 0, :].mean(axis=1)\nmean_B = series[:, 1, :].mean(axis=1)\n\nplt.figure(figsize=(6, 4))\nplt.plot(time, mean_A, label='A (mean)')\nplt.plot(time, mean_B, label='B (mean)')\nplt.xlabel('Time index')\nplt.ylabel('Molecule count')\nplt.title('Reversible reaction A \\u21cc B (Gillespie SSA, averaged over reps)')\nplt.legend()\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()\n"]}],"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"display_name":"Python","language":"python","name":"python"},"language_info":{"name":"python","mimetype":"text/x-python","file_extension":".py"}},"nbformat":4,"nbformat_minor":5}
